<h2>Unsaved Changes</h2>
<p>Warn before navigating away from a form with unsaved edits.</p>

<div class="demo-box">
  <form data-controller="unsaved-changes" data-unsaved-changes-message-value="Discard your edits?">
    <label for="unsaved-changes-title">Title</label>
    <input id="unsaved-changes-title" type="text" name="title">

    <button type="submit">Save</button>
  </form>
</div>

<p>
  Type something in the field above, then click any sidebar link (a
  Turbo Drive visit) without clicking "Save" first — a confirmation dialog
  appears, and declining it keeps you on this page. Clicking "Save" (or
  typing nothing) lets you navigate away without a prompt. This app has no
  database, so "Save" doesn't persist anything; it only demonstrates that
  submitting clears the unsaved-edits guard.
</p>

<p>
  Closing the tab or typing a new URL into the address bar while the field
  is dirty triggers the browser's own native <code>beforeunload</code>
  dialog instead — that one can't be demonstrated from within the page
  itself, since browsers require the navigation to be a real, user-initiated
  one (a script-triggered reload won't show it).
</p>
